{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794561534820,"sessionID":"ses_e2bccaf18ffeU7jQUyMBm6GhmF","part":{"partID":"prt_1d433e75d001g4YHRArHFzeiaj","sessionID":"ses_e2bccaf18ffeU7jQUyMBm6GhmF","messageID":"msg_1d433c91400174Y41pz9XXKVRo","type":"tool","id":"call_efcaa79f37ea4bc285171bf0","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/014-review-remediation/tasks.md","offset":151,"limit":120},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/014-review-remediation/tasks.md, lines 151-270\n151: ### Wave 2, lane WG13: advisor docs against code\n152: - [x] T050 Say 46 command rows (`AR`, `CMM`). Change `the 45 command rows` in `AR` and `45 rows in all` in `CMM` to 46. Verify: `rg -c \"45 (command )?rows|45 rows in all\" $AR $CMM` prints nothing and `node $DER --check | jq .counts.typescriptActive` prints 46.\n153: - [x] T051 Name the DSH prompt adapter (`ARCH`). In the adapter sentence, change `for Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin and Pi` to `for Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin, DSH and Pi` and add that the DSH adapter, `hooks/dsh/prompt-advisor.mjs`, runs the Claude handler from `runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js`. Verify: `rg -c \"hooks/dsh/prompt-advisor.mjs\" $ARCH` prints 1 and `rg -c \"Devin, DSH and Pi\" $ARCH` prints 1.\n154: - [x] T052 Give the row shape, the policy source and the gates as the code applies them (`ARM`). In the contract paragraph, give the row shape as `{ command, policy?, mutating?, ownerSkillId }`, say `mutating` is the class the renderer's child downgrade reads and replace the `COMMAND_POLICIES[command]` sentence with one saying the attached command takes its policy from `TRACKED_COMMANDS[command].policy`, the table keyed by runnable string that covers every tracked command. In the attach paragraph, replace the `system-deep-loop` sentence with one saying the prompt must type the same command or hold a `DEPTH_WORDING` phrase, and add that a prompt matching `NO_EDIT_REQUEST` gets no command from any hub. Verify: `rg -c \"COMMAND_POLICIES\" $ARM` prints nothing, `rg -c \"mutating\\?\" $ARM` prints 1, `rg -c \"TRACKED_COMMANDS\\[command\\]\\.policy\" $ARM` prints 1 and `rg -c \"NO_EDIT_REQUEST\" $ARM` prints 1.\n155: \n156: ### Wave 3, lane WG10: routing-accuracy README\n157: - [x] T053 Count and list every file in the folder (`RA`). Set `Code files` to 14 and add one row to the file table for each of `build-holdout.mjs`, `capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs`, `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs`, `derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs`, `prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs`, `reject-unknown-options.mjs`, `score-outcome-rerank.mjs`, `worktree-restore.mjs`, `ambiguity-prompts.jsonl`, `doctor-prompts.jsonl`, `holdout-prompts.jsonl`, `labeled-prompts.jsonl` and `scorer-eval-baseline.json`, each described from its header comment or, for a data file, from its fields and the script that reads it. Describe `holdout-prompts.jsonl` from `rg -l holdout-prompts $RAD` only and never open it. Verify: `rg -c \"^\\| Code files \\| 14 \\|\" $RA` prints 1 and ``rg -c '^\\| `[^`]+\\.(mjs|py|jsonl|json)` \\|' $RA`` prints 21.\n158: - [x] T054 Describe exit 3, the arena refusal and the allowlisted environment in the runner row (`RA`). Say exit 3 (`head_moved`) ends the arm without the restore, because a commit or a stash made during the turn is not the runner's to discard, add `arena_unprepared` to the exit 2 refusals and add that a live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics and each runtime's own prefixes plus the GitHub and git overrides. Verify: `rg -c \"head_moved\" $RA` prints at least 1, `rg -c \"arena_unprepared\" $RA` prints 1 and `rg -c \"allowlist\" $RA` prints at least 1.\n159: - [x] T055 Say what `offerReached` counts and that the baseline publishes no holdout row (`RA`). In the scorer row, replace `offerReached counts offers on a turn whose brief arrived` with `offerReached counts offers whose brief was not shown missing, so a row whose brief state is unknown counts`. In the capture row, add that its `rows`, `attachedRows` and `typedRows` maps leave out every holdout row and only holdout counts are published. Verify: `rg -c \"whose brief arrived\" $RA` prints nothing, `rg -c \"not shown missing\" $RA` prints 1 and `rg -c \"leave out every holdout row\" $RA` prints 1.\n160: \n161: ### Wave 4, verifier follow-ups\n162: - [ ] T062 Replace the stderr case that passes without the cap (`SUBT`). The case `bounds stderr from a child that floods it and exits non-zero` passes with or without the cap in `SUB`, because `runAdvisorSubprocess` returns stderr through `sanitizeStderr`, which already cuts it to 240 characters. The one stderr reading that depends on the cap is the busy check: `runAdvisorSubprocess` matches `SQLITE_BUSY_PATTERN` against the collected stderr and retries once on a match, so a busy marker written after `MAX_OUTPUT_CHARS` characters is seen only when stderr is not capped. Replace that case with `it('stops reading stderr at the cap, so a busy marker past it is not seen', ...)`. Its script, written with the file's `writeScript`, waits for stdin to end as the other case does, writes `'e'.repeat(1024 * 1024)` and then `'\\ndatabase is locked\\n'` to stderr and sets `process.exitCode = 1`. Call `runAdvisorSubprocess` with the same options as the stdout case and expect `result.ok` to be `false`, `result.errorCode` to be `'NON_ZERO_EXIT'` and `result.retriesAttempted` to be `0`. Without the cap the marker is read, the call retries once and returns `SQLITE_BUSY_EXHAUSTED` with `retriesAttempted` 1. Keep the stdout case, keep the local `MAX_OUTPUT_CHARS` only if a case still reads it and change nothing in `SUB`. The cap is already in, so do not remove it to watch the case fail and do not touch git state: say in your report why the case would fail without the cap. Add a comment above the case saying the returned stderr is cut short whatever the cap, so the retry decision is the one reading that shows where collection stopped. Verify: `VX tests/subprocess-output-cap.vitest.ts` reports `2 passed (2)`, `rg -c \"database is locked\" $SUBT` prints 1 and `rg -c \"toBeLessThanOrEqual\" $SUBT` prints nothing.\n163: - [ ] T063 Restore the bench header and make only the one header change asked for (`BENCH`). The five comment lines under the module banner were rewritten into eight when only one line needed to change. Run `git -c core.fsmonitor=false show HEAD:.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/bench/hook-brief-signal-noise.bench.ts | sed -n 4,8p` and put those five lines back in place of the eight comment lines that now sit between the banner and the first `import`. Then change only the first of them, `// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (claude/opencode),`, to `// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (the iteration measured Claude and OpenCode, and the rest read not-recorded),`. Leave the other four restored lines as `HEAD` has them. Put the sentence about invented readings on its own comment line directly above the `pi:` row of `SEVEN_AXIS_MATRIX`, indented as the rows are: `// An invented axis reading would pass for a measured one, so a runtime nobody probed reads not-recorded.` Keep the five `not-recorded` rows and the realigned `claude` and `opencode` rows as they are. Verify: `VB` reports `4 passed (4)`, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- $BENCH | rg '^[-+]//'` prints exactly the two lines `-// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (claude/opencode),` and `+// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (the iteration measured Claude and OpenCode, and the rest read not-recorded),` and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- $BENCH | rg -c '^\\+\\s+// An invented axis reading'` prints 1.\n164: - [ ] T064 Count a hook as wired only when `node` stands where a shell starts a command (`HW`, `HWT`). Test first: in `HWT`, add three cases built with `writeClaudeSettings`, each with a `UserPromptSubmit` group holding one `{ type: 'command', command: <cmd> }` hook. With `node ${CLAUDE_MARKER}` the check prints `ok claude` and exits 0. With `/opt/homebrew/bin/node ${CLAUDE_MARKER}` it prints `ok claude` and exits 0. With `echo node ${CLAUDE_MARKER}` it prints `hook-missing claude .claude/settings.json` and exits 1. Today the first passes, the second fails on `hook-missing` and the third fails on `ok claude`. Then rewrite `runsMarker`. A token, after `stripQuotes`, is a `node` executable when it equals `node` or ends with `/node`. It counts only at a command position: the first token of the command, a token right after `&&`, `||`, `;` or `|`, or the token right after `-c` when the token before `-c` is `bash` or `sh`. Report a match when such a token is followed by a token ending in the runtime's marker. These positions cover every real shape in this repository: `.cursor/hooks.json` starts its command with `node`, and `.claude/settings.json`, `.codex/hooks.json` and `.devin/hooks.v1.json` run `bash -c 'cd \"...\" && node <marker> || ...'`. Add a comment above `runsMarker` saying an argument that only names `node`, as `echo node <script>` does, runs nothing, so `node` must stand where a shell starts a command. Verify: before the change `NT $HWT` reports `fail 2`, after it `NT $HWT` reports `fail 0`, and `node $HW --root .` prints `ok claude`, `ok codex`, `ok cursor` and `ok devin` and exits 0.\n165: - [ ] T065 Keep a failed restore inside the write-fault branch from escaping `main` (`EMB`, `EMBT`). `restoreFromBackup` never calls the write hook, because it restores with `fs.copyFileSync` and `fs.rmSync`, so the test makes its own read of the backup's `manifest.json` fail. Test first: in `EMBT`, add a case after `a write that throws mid-apply restores the backup and exits 3`, built like it on the `claude-only` fixture with `--create-router`. Take `backup` from the plan's output with `field(planned, 'backup')` and pass that output to `trackBackup(t, ...)`, as the damaged-backup case does. On its second call the write hook writes `not json` to `path.join(backup, 'manifest.json')` and then throws the same `EACCES` error the existing case throws. Expect `embed.main` to return 3 and the output to hold `ERROR: writing`, `ERROR: the restore failed: `, a line starting `backup:`, a line starting `undo:` and `STATUS=FAIL`, and not to hold `restored:`. Today `main` throws the `SyntaxError` from `JSON.parse`. Then in the `catch` of `commandApply`, wrap the `restoreFromBackup` call in its own `try` and `catch`, and on a throw print `${TAG} ERROR: the restore failed: <message>` with `output.warn`. The `backup:`, `undo:` and `STATUS=FAIL` lines and `return 3` follow on both paths as they do today. Add a comment saying the backup and undo lines are the operator's only way back once the restore itself fails, so a second fault must not swallow them. Verify: before the change `NT $EMBT` reports `fail 1` on the new case, after it `NT $EMBT` reports `fail 0` and `node $PL/probe-embed-write-fault.cjs` still prints `main returned 3`.\n166: - [ ] T066 Move the exit 3 comment beside the code it explains (`EMB`). The four comment lines that start `// A write that throws partway through is neither a run that wrote nothing nor a finished` sit alone between `USAGE` and the `3. ERRORS` section banner. Move them, wording unchanged and indented two spaces, to sit directly above the `try {` that wraps the write loop in `commandApply`, below `let writing = null;` with one blank line between that line and the comment. Leave one blank line between `].join('\\n');` and the section banner where they were. Change no code. Verify: `node --check $EMB` exits 0, `NT $EMBT` reports `fail 0`, `rg -n -A1 \"of its own rather than borrowing one the usage already spends on another meaning\" $EMB` prints the comment line and then a line ending `try {`, and that comment line's number is greater than the one `rg -n \"^function commandApply\" $EMB` prints.\n167: - [ ] T067 Say which status wins when the engine and the hook check disagree (`DSC`, `DSCT`). Test first: in `DSCT`, add one assertion to the case `the symlink output block maps the hook-wiring check exits to WARN and FAIL`: `assert.match(SYMLINK_CHECK.output, /When the engine and the hook-wiring check end on different statuses, the worse one wins: FAIL over WARN over OK\\./)`. Then add the line `When the engine and the hook-wiring check end on different statuses, the worse one wins: FAIL over WARN over OK.` as the last line of the `output: |` block in `DSC`, indented two spaces as the lines above it are. Verify: before the change `NT $DSCT` reports `fail 1`, after it `NT $DSCT` and `NT $DS/tests/doctor-symlink-contract.test.cjs` report `fail 0`.\n168: - [ ] T068 Run the CLI when it is launched through a symlink (`R`, `S`, `BG`). Both files compute `invokedDirectly` as `resolve(process.argv[1]) === fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)`. Node gives a linked entry file its real path in `import.meta.url` but leaves `process.argv[1]` as typed, so a launch through a link, for example under `.claude/skills/`, prints nothing and exits 0. Test first: in `BG`, add `describe('entry guard', ...)` with one case. It makes a temporary folder with `mkdtempSync`, links `<tmp>/runner.mjs` to `R` and `<tmp>/scorer.mjs` to `S` with `symlinkSync` and removes the folder in `finally`. Find each real path with `fileURLToPath(new URL('../scripts/routing-accuracy/<file name>', import.meta.url))`, and add `symlinkSync` to the `node:fs` import and `fileURLToPath` from `node:url`. Run each link with `spawnSync(process.execPath, [<link>, ...args], { encoding: 'utf8' })`. The runner link with `['--list', 'g2']` must exit 0 with the first stdout line `g2-deep-research-ask`. The scorer link with no arguments must exit 2 with stderr holding `--runtime is required with --locator`. Neither starts a runtime. Today both fail, because each link prints nothing and exits 0. Then in both files compute `invokedDirectly` by comparing `realpathSync` of `process.argv[1]` with `realpathSync` of `fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)`, and treat a throw from `realpathSync`, as for an `argv[1]` that names no file, as not invoked directly. `R` already imports `realpathSync` and `S` must add it to its `node:fs` import. Add one sentence to each file's comment above `invokedDirectly` saying the entry path is compared after resolving links, because Node names a linked entry by its real path. Verify: before the change `VX tests/command-behaviour-guards.vitest.ts` reports 1 failed, after it 0 failed, `node --check $R` and `node --check $S` exit 0 and `G2SUM` prints its planning line. Then set `L=\"$(mktemp -d)\"`, run `ln -s \"$PWD/$R\" \"$L/r.mjs\"` and `ln -s \"$PWD/$S\" \"$L/s.mjs\"`, ch... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n169: - [ ] T069 Move the arena marker comment beside the write (`ARB`). The three comment lines starting `// The runner refuses a live arm in a checkout without this file` sit above `const MARK = 'skilled-behaviour-arena';`. Move them, wording unchanged and indented two spaces, to sit directly above the `writeFileSync` call in `buildArena` that writes the marker under `join(dest, '.git', MARK)`. Leave one blank line between the `GIT_PREFIX` constant and the `MARK` constant. Change no code. Verify: `node --check $ARB` exits 0, `rg -c \"skilled-behaviour-arena\" $ARB` prints 1 and `rg -n -A1 \"its copied build trees\\.\" $ARB` prints the comment line and then the line holding `writeFileSync(join(dest, '.git', MARK)`.\n170: - [ ] T070 Read the Pi session header from the contained path (`$SR/pi.mjs`, `$SRT/pi.test.mjs`, `$SR/*.d.mts`, `$SR/*.d.mts.map`). `diskResult` opens the session file for its header with `headerOf(filePath)` before `guardSessionPath` checks that the path stays inside the store, so a session file that links out of the store is opened before the refusal. Test first: in `$SRT/pi.test.mjs`, add `test('a session file linked outside the store is refused without opening it', ...)` inside `withTempAsync('pi-outside', ...)`. Build `piSession({ root, projectDir })` after making `projectDir` as the other cases do. Write `join(root, 'outside-pi.jsonl')` with `writeJsonl`, holding the header `{ type: 'session', version: 3, id: SESSION, timestamp: '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z', cwd: projectDir }` and one user `message` row shaped like the fixture's `pi-u1` row. Replace `fixture.filePath` with a link to it, `rmSync(fixture.filePath)` and then `symlinkSync(outside, fixture.filePath)`. Start `trackFileOpens()`, call `readSession({ runtime: 'pi', sessionId: SESSION, ...fixture.options })` and call `tracker.restore()` in `finally`. Expect `ok` to be `false`, `code` to be `session_mismatch` and `tracker.opened` to hold neither `fixture.filePath` nor the outside path. Import `rmSync` and `symlinkSync` from `node:fs` and `trackFileOpens` and `writeJsonl` from `./helpers.mjs`. Today the code matches and the open check fails, because `headerOf` opens the link. Then in `diskResult`, right after `filePath` is built, call `resolveInside({ path: filePath, root })` and return its result when it is not `ok`. Read the header with `headerOf(contained.path)` and pass `contained.path` as `path` to `guardSessionPath`. Import `resolveInside` beside `guardSessionPath` from `./identity.mjs`. Add a comment saying the header is read from the path the containment check resolved, so a link out of the store is refused before any byte of it is read. Then run `SRD` and do not edit a declaration by hand. Verify: ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n171: - [ ] T071 Name the owner skill, not the raw row id, on a needs-a-mode head (`RD`, `GT`). A `needsMode` recommendation whose id has a generated command row, such as `command-create-agent`, renders `command-create-agent (needs a mode)`, because `commandLabel` returns `null` for it and the head falls back to the raw id. Test first: in `GT`, tighten the case `renders needs a mode even when the id has a command row` so it also expects the head to hold `use sk-doc (needs a mode)` and not to hold `command-`. Today it fails, because the head reads `use command-create-agent (needs a mode)`. Then in `renderAdvisorBrief`, change the `topLabel` fallback from `sanitizeSkillLabel(metadataSkillLabel(result) ?? top.skill)` to `sanitizeSkillLabel(metadataSkillLabel(result) ?? (top.needsMode === true ? commandRowFor(top.skill)?.ownerSkillId : undefined) ?? top.skill)`. Add a comment saying a recommendation that needs a mode is named by the skill that owns its row, because the row id is an internal key the reader cannot run. Verify: before the change `VX tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` reports 1 failed, after it 0 failed, `VX tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n172: - [ ] T072 Fail the holdout check on a corpus that marks no holdout row (`SLT`). Start only after T041 ends, because WG9 owns this file until then. In the case `publishes no holdout row`, add `expect(holdoutIds.size, 'the corpus marks no holdout row, so this check would pass without testing anything').toBeGreaterThan(0)` right after `holdoutIds` is built and before the key checks. Add a comment saying an empty set would make the check pass on a corpus that no longer marks its holdout rows. It passes today and fails the day the `split` field goes. Read only `id` and `split` from `CORPUS` and never print a holdout prompt. Verify: `VX tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed and `rg -c \"holdoutIds.size\" $SLT` prints 1.\n173: - [ ] T073 Tighten three README sentences the verifier found loose (`RA`). Start only after T055 ends, because WG10 owns this file until then. In the runner row, replace the sentence `A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics and each runtime's own prefixes plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no other credential in the operator's shell reaches it.` with `A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics, the locale and XDG prefixes, each runtime's own prefixes and the Skilled switch prefixes, plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no GitHub token or git credential helper from the operator's shell reaches it.` In the capture row, change `the command attached to each row` to `the command attached to each published row`. In the `scorer-eval-baseline.json` row, change ``the fixture hashes and `metrics` for the full corpus, holdout, ambiguity slice, gold-none false fire and intent buckets`` to ``the fixture hashes and `metrics` for the full corpus, holdout, ambiguity slice, gold-none false fire, intent buckets and unknown count``, and add `` `capturedAtSha`,`` after `` `capturedAt`,`` in the same row. Change nothing else in the file. Verify: `rg -c \"the locale and XDG prefixes\" $RA` prints 1, `rg -c \"no other credential\" $RA` prints nothing, `rg -c \"attached to each published row\" $RA` prints 1, `rg -c \"capturedAtSha\" $RA` prints at least 1, `rg -c \"intent buckets and unknown count\" $RA` prints 1 and ``rg -c '^\\| `[^`]+\\.(mjs|py|jsonl|json)` \\|' $RA`` prints 21.\n174: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n175: \n176: ---\n177: \n178: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n179: ## Phase 3: Verification\n180: \n181: - [ ] T056 [C] Rebuild and run every suite (`$SP/after.txt`). Run `npm run build` in `ADV`, then `VALL`, `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1 npx vitest run tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts tests/hooks/skill-advisor-cli-fallback-envelope.vitest.ts tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts` in `ADV`, `VB`, `DALL` and `VSR`, and write each command with its result lines into `after.txt`. Verify: the build exits 0, `VALL` reports 0 failed with more passed tests than `$SP/before.txt`, the marker run reports 0 failed, `VB` reports `4 passed (4)` and `DALL` and `VSR` report `fail 0`.\n182: - [ ] T057 [C] Hold every floor (`$SP/after.txt`). Run `FLOOR`, `EVAL`, `TC`, `SRD --check`, `DERIVE`, `RV`, `SRM`, `G2SUM` and `HOLD`, write each beside its before line and end the file with `RESULT: every floor holds` only when it does. Verify: no `FLOOR` value and no `EVAL` value in its first three places is lower than its before line, the fourth `EVAL` value is no higher than 5, `TC`, `RV` and `SRM` exit 0, `SRD --check` prints `current`, `DERIVE` prints `fresh`, `G2SUM` equals its before line and `HOLD` prints `\"rows\":0`.\n183: - [ ] T058 [C] Rerun the reproductions (`$SP/repro-after.txt`). Run the six probes in `PL` after the build and append their output. Verify: the file holds `main returned 3`, `restored line: true`, `after: CLAUDE.md`, `plan exit=2`, a `ci.yml` line with equal digests, `undefined` on the no-edit, `/deep:ai-council` and both file-name lines of `probe-attach.mts`, `'COMMAND_POLICIES' in module -> false`, `(needs a mode)` on the first `probe-render.mjs` line and `(parent-named only)` on its second, and `probe-postwrite-foreign.cjs` prints 15 `apply exit 0` lines.\n184: - [ ] T059 [C] Build one arena and read its marker (`$SP/arena-smoke.txt`). Run `node $ARB --source . --dest <a fresh folder under mktemp -d>/arena`, then `test -f <arena>/.git/skilled-behaviour-arena` and `node -e \"import('./$R').then((m) => console.log(JSON.stringify(m.liveArmRefusal(process.argv[1]))))\" <arena>`, write the outputs to `arena-smoke.txt` and remove the temporary folder. Start no runtime in it. Verify: the builder prints `\"ok\":true`, the `test -f` exits 0, the refusal prints `null` and `test -e <the temporary folder>` exits 1 at the end.\n185: - [ ] T060 [C] Check the boundaries. Verify: `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- AGENTS.md specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/spec.md $ADV/lib/scorer/text.ts` exits 0, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- .skilled | rg \"^\\+.*(//|#|\\*).*(specs/|REQ-0|ADR-0|T0[0-9]{2}|phase [0-9]|WG[0-9]|DR[0-9]-P|R[0-9]-P[0-9]|iter[0-9]-P|Q[0-9]-)\"` prints nothing, the same `rg` over `CAG`, `BG`, `SUBT` and `DSCT` prints nothing, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --stat HEAD -- '*package.json' '*package-lock.json'` prints nothing, `rg -n \"COMMAND_POLICIES\" .skilled --glob '!**/node_modules/**'` prints nothing and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --short -- .skilled` lists only files named in this phase's tasks.\n186: - [ ] T061 [C] Write `implementation-summary.md` and mark each row in `acceptance-criteria.md` with its evidence (`implementation-summary.md`, `acceptance-criteria.md`). Run `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/repair-derived.cjs --folder <this folder> --apply` first. Verify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <this folder> --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED`.\n187: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n188: \n189: ---\n190: \n191: <!-- ANCHOR:completion -->\n192: ## Completion Criteria\n193: \n194: - [ ] All tasks marked `[x]`\n195: - [ ] No `[B]` blocked tasks remaining\n196: - [ ] `$SP/after.txt` ends `RESULT: every floor holds` and `$SP/repro-after.txt` holds every fixed output T058 names\n197: <!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n198: \n199: ---\n200: \n201: <!-- ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n202: ## Cross-References\n203: \n204: - **Specification**: See `spec.md`\n205: - **Plan**: See `plan.md`\n206: - **Acceptance criteria**: See `acceptance-criteria.md`\n207: - **Review report**: `../review/review-report.md`\n208: <!-- /ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n209: \n210: ---\n211: \n212: ## Verification Checklist\n213: \n214: <!-- ANCHOR:protocol -->\n215: ## Verification Protocol\n216: \n217: | Priority | Handling | Completion Impact |\n218: |----------|----------|-------------------|\n219: | **[P0]** | HARD BLOCKER | Cannot claim done until complete |\n220: | **[P1]** | Required | Must complete OR get user approval |\n221: | **[P2]** | Optional | Can defer with documented reason |\n222: \n223: A blocked task gets `[B]` and one line naming what blocks it. A moved floor blocks the phase.\n224: <!-- /ANCHOR:protocol -->\n225: \n226: ---\n227: \n228: <!-- ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n229: ## Pre-Implementation\n230: \n231: - [ ] CHK-001 [P0] The before numbers and the reproductions are in `scratch/` (T001, T002), and `rg -c \"^order:\" scratch/before.txt` prints 1 (T003).\n232: - [ ] CHK-002 [P0] Each task line ends with a verify clause: `rg -c '^- \\[[ xB]\\] T[0-9].*Verify: ' tasks.md` and `rg -c '^- \\[[ xB]\\] T[0-9]' tasks.md` print the same number.\n233: <!-- /ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n234: \n235: ---\n236: \n237: <!-- ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n238: ## Code Quality\n239: \n240: - [ ] CHK-010 [P0] `TC` exits 0 and `node --check` passes on every changed `.mjs` and `.cjs` file.\n241: - [ ] CHK-011 [P0] No code comment names a spec path, a phase number, a task id, a finding id, an ADR or a REQ id (T060).\n242: - [ ] CHK-012 [P0] The generated block in `CA`, the baseline and the reader declarations are written by their generators only: `DERIVE` prints `fresh`, `SRD --check` prints `current` and `SLT` passes on the recaptured baseline.\n243: - [ ] CHK-013 [P1] `CA` still has no import: `rg -c \"^import\" $CA` prints nothing.\n244: <!-- /ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n245: \n246: ---\n247: \n248: <!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n249: ## Testing Checklist\n250: \n251: - [ ] CHK-020 [P0] Every floor holds (T057): `scratch/after.txt` ends `RESULT: every floor holds`.\n252: - [ ] CHK-021 [P0] Each fix's verify recorded its test failing before the change and passing after, except the cases the spec names as passing today.\n253: - [ ] CHK-022 [P0] The two former P1 reproductions print the fixed output (T058).\n254: - [ ] CHK-023 [P0] The renderer tests pass under `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1` (T056).\n255: - [ ] CHK-024 [P1] One arena built by the builder carries the marker and passes `liveArmRefusal` (T059).\n256: <!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n257: \n258: ---\n259: \n260: <!-- ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n261: ## FIX ADDENDUM: FIX COMPLETENESS\n262: \n263: - [ ] CHK-FIX-001 [P0] Each finding has a class: embed write fault and restore `algorithmic`, linked router or mirror `class-of-bug` (the root-document rule extended to every written path), foreign failure `instance-only`, the attach guards `cross-consumer` (attach path, scorer, capture), `COMMAND_POLICIES` `dead generated surface`, renderer heads `instance-only`, renderer tests `test-isolation`, env allowlist and arena marker `class-of-bug`, g2 labels `duplicated source of truth`, the check, workflow and README findings `matrix/evidence` or doc drift, the readers `instance-only` except the containment path `class-of-bug` across four readers.\n264: - [ ] CHK-FIX-002 [P0] Same-class producers: `rg -n \"NO_EDIT_REQUEST\" $ADV/lib` lists `CA` and `FU` only, with `scorer/text.ts` holding the recorded third copy, and `rg -n \"isSymlink\\(\" $EMB` lists the root, router, mirror and `CLAUDE.md` checks.\n265: - [ ] CHK-FIX-003 [P0] Consumers of every changed symbol are listed in `plan.md` and the consumer `rg` in its required inventories prints no unlisted file (T060).\n266: - [ ] CHK-FIX-004 [P0] The path and parser fixes carry adversarial cases: a link to an in-root file, a partial and a complete linked router, a file name with `.` and with `/`, a sentence-ending period and a comma, a typo `/deep:` command, a router row with a literal pipe and a marker named only in a description.\n267: - [ ] CHK-FIX-005 [P1] The matrix axes in `plan.md` each have their rows in the tests T004 to T011, T021, T022, T027 and T031 add.\n268: - [ ] CHK-FIX-006 [P1] The hostile env variant ran: the renderer tests under `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1` and the env allowlist test with secrets stubbed.\n269: - [ ] CHK-FIX-007 [P1] Evidence names the commit it ran on: `scratch/after.txt` and `implementation-summary.md` name the base commit with the changes in the working tree.\n270: <!-- /ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 271]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/014-review-remediation/tasks.md, lines 151-270\n151: ### Wave 2, lane WG13: advisor docs against code\n152: - [x] T050 Say 46 command rows (`AR`, `CMM`). Change `the 45 command rows` in `AR` and `45 rows in all` in `CMM` to 46. Verify: `rg -c \"45 (command )?rows|45 rows in all\" $AR $CMM` prints nothing and `node $DER --check | jq .counts.typescriptActive` prints 46.\n153: - [x] T051 Name the DSH prompt adapter (`ARCH`). In the adapter sentence, change `for Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin and Pi` to `for Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin, DSH and Pi` and add that the DSH adapter, `hooks/dsh/prompt-advisor.mjs`, runs the Claude handler from `runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js`. Verify: `rg -c \"hooks/dsh/prompt-advisor.mjs\" $ARCH` prints 1 and `rg -c \"Devin, DSH and Pi\" $ARCH` prints 1.\n154: - [x] T052 Give the row shape, the policy source and the gates as the code applies them (`ARM`). In the contract paragraph, give the row shape as `{ command, policy?, mutating?, ownerSkillId }`, say `mutating` is the class the renderer's child downgrade reads and replace the `COMMAND_POLICIES[command]` sentence with one saying the attached command takes its policy from `TRACKED_COMMANDS[command].policy`, the table keyed by runnable string that covers every tracked command. In the attach paragraph, replace the `system-deep-loop` sentence with one saying the prompt must type the same command or hold a `DEPTH_WORDING` phrase, and add that a prompt matching `NO_EDIT_REQUEST` gets no command from any hub. Verify: `rg -c \"COMMAND_POLICIES\" $ARM` prints nothing, `rg -c \"mutating\\?\" $ARM` prints 1, `rg -c \"TRACKED_COMMANDS\\[command\\]\\.policy\" $ARM` prints 1 and `rg -c \"NO_EDIT_REQUEST\" $ARM` prints 1.\n155: \n156: ### Wave 3, lane WG10: routing-accuracy README\n157: - [x] T053 Count and list every file in the folder (`RA`). Set `Code files` to 14 and add one row to the file table for each of `build-holdout.mjs`, `capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs`, `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs`, `derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs`, `prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs`, `reject-unknown-options.mjs`, `score-outcome-rerank.mjs`, `worktree-restore.mjs`, `ambiguity-prompts.jsonl`, `doctor-prompts.jsonl`, `holdout-prompts.jsonl`, `labeled-prompts.jsonl` and `scorer-eval-baseline.json`, each described from its header comment or, for a data file, from its fields and the script that reads it. Describe `holdout-prompts.jsonl` from `rg -l holdout-prompts $RAD` only and never open it. Verify: `rg -c \"^\\| Code files \\| 14 \\|\" $RA` prints 1 and ``rg -c '^\\| `[^`]+\\.(mjs|py|jsonl|json)` \\|' $RA`` prints 21.\n158: - [x] T054 Describe exit 3, the arena refusal and the allowlisted environment in the runner row (`RA`). Say exit 3 (`head_moved`) ends the arm without the restore, because a commit or a stash made during the turn is not the runner's to discard, add `arena_unprepared` to the exit 2 refusals and add that a live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics and each runtime's own prefixes plus the GitHub and git overrides. Verify: `rg -c \"head_moved\" $RA` prints at least 1, `rg -c \"arena_unprepared\" $RA` prints 1 and `rg -c \"allowlist\" $RA` prints at least 1.\n159: - [x] T055 Say what `offerReached` counts and that the baseline publishes no holdout row (`RA`). In the scorer row, replace `offerReached counts offers on a turn whose brief arrived` with `offerReached counts offers whose brief was not shown missing, so a row whose brief state is unknown counts`. In the capture row, add that its `rows`, `attachedRows` and `typedRows` maps leave out every holdout row and only holdout counts are published. Verify: `rg -c \"whose brief arrived\" $RA` prints nothing, `rg -c \"not shown missing\" $RA` prints 1 and `rg -c \"leave out every holdout row\" $RA` prints 1.\n160: \n161: ### Wave 4, verifier follow-ups\n162: - [ ] T062 Replace the stderr case that passes without the cap (`SUBT`). The case `bounds stderr from a child that floods it and exits non-zero` passes with or without the cap in `SUB`, because `runAdvisorSubprocess` returns stderr through `sanitizeStderr`, which already cuts it to 240 characters. The one stderr reading that depends on the cap is the busy check: `runAdvisorSubprocess` matches `SQLITE_BUSY_PATTERN` against the collected stderr and retries once on a match, so a busy marker written after `MAX_OUTPUT_CHARS` characters is seen only when stderr is not capped. Replace that case with `it('stops reading stderr at the cap, so a busy marker past it is not seen', ...)`. Its script, written with the file's `writeScript`, waits for stdin to end as the other case does, writes `'e'.repeat(1024 * 1024)` and then `'\\ndatabase is locked\\n'` to stderr and sets `process.exitCode = 1`. Call `runAdvisorSubprocess` with the same options as the stdout case and expect `result.ok` to be `false`, `result.errorCode` to be `'NON_ZERO_EXIT'` and `result.retriesAttempted` to be `0`. Without the cap the marker is read, the call retries once and returns `SQLITE_BUSY_EXHAUSTED` with `retriesAttempted` 1. Keep the stdout case, keep the local `MAX_OUTPUT_CHARS` only if a case still reads it and change nothing in `SUB`. The cap is already in, so do not remove it to watch the case fail and do not touch git state: say in your report why the case would fail without the cap. Add a comment above the case saying the returned stderr is cut short whatever the cap, so the retry decision is the one reading that shows where collection stopped. Verify: `VX tests/subprocess-output-cap.vitest.ts` reports `2 passed (2)`, `rg -c \"database is locked\" $SUBT` prints 1 and `rg -c \"toBeLessThanOrEqual\" $SUBT` prints nothing.\n163: - [ ] T063 Restore the bench header and make only the one header change asked for (`BENCH`). The five comment lines under the module banner were rewritten into eight when only one line needed to change. Run `git -c core.fsmonitor=false show HEAD:.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/bench/hook-brief-signal-noise.bench.ts | sed -n 4,8p` and put those five lines back in place of the eight comment lines that now sit between the banner and the first `import`. Then change only the first of them, `// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (claude/opencode),`, to `// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (the iteration measured Claude and OpenCode, and the rest read not-recorded),`. Leave the other four restored lines as `HEAD` has them. Put the sentence about invented readings on its own comment line directly above the `pi:` row of `SEVEN_AXIS_MATRIX`, indented as the rows are: `// An invented axis reading would pass for a measured one, so a runtime nobody probed reads not-recorded.` Keep the five `not-recorded` rows and the realigned `claude` and `opencode` rows as they are. Verify: `VB` reports `4 passed (4)`, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- $BENCH | rg '^[-+]//'` prints exactly the two lines `-// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (claude/opencode),` and `+// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (the iteration measured Claude and OpenCode, and the rest read not-recorded),` and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- $BENCH | rg -c '^\\+\\s+// An invented axis reading'` prints 1.\n164: - [ ] T064 Count a hook as wired only when `node` stands where a shell starts a command (`HW`, `HWT`). Test first: in `HWT`, add three cases built with `writeClaudeSettings`, each with a `UserPromptSubmit` group holding one `{ type: 'command', command: <cmd> }` hook. With `node ${CLAUDE_MARKER}` the check prints `ok claude` and exits 0. With `/opt/homebrew/bin/node ${CLAUDE_MARKER}` it prints `ok claude` and exits 0. With `echo node ${CLAUDE_MARKER}` it prints `hook-missing claude .claude/settings.json` and exits 1. Today the first passes, the second fails on `hook-missing` and the third fails on `ok claude`. Then rewrite `runsMarker`. A token, after `stripQuotes`, is a `node` executable when it equals `node` or ends with `/node`. It counts only at a command position: the first token of the command, a token right after `&&`, `||`, `;` or `|`, or the token right after `-c` when the token before `-c` is `bash` or `sh`. Report a match when such a token is followed by a token ending in the runtime's marker. These positions cover every real shape in this repository: `.cursor/hooks.json` starts its command with `node`, and `.claude/settings.json`, `.codex/hooks.json` and `.devin/hooks.v1.json` run `bash -c 'cd \"...\" && node <marker> || ...'`. Add a comment above `runsMarker` saying an argument that only names `node`, as `echo node <script>` does, runs nothing, so `node` must stand where a shell starts a command. Verify: before the change `NT $HWT` reports `fail 2`, after it `NT $HWT` reports `fail 0`, and `node $HW --root .` prints `ok claude`, `ok codex`, `ok cursor` and `ok devin` and exits 0.\n165: - [ ] T065 Keep a failed restore inside the write-fault branch from escaping `main` (`EMB`, `EMBT`). `restoreFromBackup` never calls the write hook, because it restores with `fs.copyFileSync` and `fs.rmSync`, so the test makes its own read of the backup's `manifest.json` fail. Test first: in `EMBT`, add a case after `a write that throws mid-apply restores the backup and exits 3`, built like it on the `claude-only` fixture with `--create-router`. Take `backup` from the plan's output with `field(planned, 'backup')` and pass that output to `trackBackup(t, ...)`, as the damaged-backup case does. On its second call the write hook writes `not json` to `path.join(backup, 'manifest.json')` and then throws the same `EACCES` error the existing case throws. Expect `embed.main` to return 3 and the output to hold `ERROR: writing`, `ERROR: the restore failed: `, a line starting `backup:`, a line starting `undo:` and `STATUS=FAIL`, and not to hold `restored:`. Today `main` throws the `SyntaxError` from `JSON.parse`. Then in the `catch` of `commandApply`, wrap the `restoreFromBackup` call in its own `try` and `catch`, and on a throw print `${TAG} ERROR: the restore failed: <message>` with `output.warn`. The `backup:`, `undo:` and `STATUS=FAIL` lines and `return 3` follow on both paths as they do today. Add a comment saying the backup and undo lines are the operator's only way back once the restore itself fails, so a second fault must not swallow them. Verify: before the change `NT $EMBT` reports `fail 1` on the new case, after it `NT $EMBT` reports `fail 0` and `node $PL/probe-embed-write-fault.cjs` still prints `main returned 3`.\n166: - [ ] T066 Move the exit 3 comment beside the code it explains (`EMB`). The four comment lines that start `// A write that throws partway through is neither a run that wrote nothing nor a finished` sit alone between `USAGE` and the `3. ERRORS` section banner. Move them, wording unchanged and indented two spaces, to sit directly above the `try {` that wraps the write loop in `commandApply`, below `let writing = null;` with one blank line between that line and the comment. Leave one blank line between `].join('\\n');` and the section banner where they were. Change no code. Verify: `node --check $EMB` exits 0, `NT $EMBT` reports `fail 0`, `rg -n -A1 \"of its own rather than borrowing one the usage already spends on another meaning\" $EMB` prints the comment line and then a line ending `try {`, and that comment line's number is greater than the one `rg -n \"^function commandApply\" $EMB` prints.\n167: - [ ] T067 Say which status wins when the engine and the hook check disagree (`DSC`, `DSCT`). Test first: in `DSCT`, add one assertion to the case `the symlink output block maps the hook-wiring check exits to WARN and FAIL`: `assert.match(SYMLINK_CHECK.output, /When the engine and the hook-wiring check end on different statuses, the worse one wins: FAIL over WARN over OK\\./)`. Then add the line `When the engine and the hook-wiring check end on different statuses, the worse one wins: FAIL over WARN over OK.` as the last line of the `output: |` block in `DSC`, indented two spaces as the lines above it are. Verify: before the change `NT $DSCT` reports `fail 1`, after it `NT $DSCT` and `NT $DS/tests/doctor-symlink-contract.test.cjs` report `fail 0`.\n168: - [ ] T068 Run the CLI when it is launched through a symlink (`R`, `S`, `BG`). Both files compute `invokedDirectly` as `resolve(process.argv[1]) === fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)`. Node gives a linked entry file its real path in `import.meta.url` but leaves `process.argv[1]` as typed, so a launch through a link, for example under `.claude/skills/`, prints nothing and exits 0. Test first: in `BG`, add `describe('entry guard', ...)` with one case. It makes a temporary folder with `mkdtempSync`, links `<tmp>/runner.mjs` to `R` and `<tmp>/scorer.mjs` to `S` with `symlinkSync` and removes the folder in `finally`. Find each real path with `fileURLToPath(new URL('../scripts/routing-accuracy/<file name>', import.meta.url))`, and add `symlinkSync` to the `node:fs` import and `fileURLToPath` from `node:url`. Run each link with `spawnSync(process.execPath, [<link>, ...args], { encoding: 'utf8' })`. The runner link with `['--list', 'g2']` must exit 0 with the first stdout line `g2-deep-research-ask`. The scorer link with no arguments must exit 2 with stderr holding `--runtime is required with --locator`. Neither starts a runtime. Today both fail, because each link prints nothing and exits 0. Then in both files compute `invokedDirectly` by comparing `realpathSync` of `process.argv[1]` with `realpathSync` of `fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)`, and treat a throw from `realpathSync`, as for an `argv[1]` that names no file, as not invoked directly. `R` already imports `realpathSync` and `S` must add it to its `node:fs` import. Add one sentence to each file's comment above `invokedDirectly` saying the entry path is compared after resolving links, because Node names a linked entry by its real path. Verify: before the change `VX tests/command-behaviour-guards.vitest.ts` reports 1 failed, after it 0 failed, `node --check $R` and `node --check $S` exit 0 and `G2SUM` prints its planning line. Then set `L=\"$(mktemp -d)\"`, run `ln -s \"$PWD/$R\" \"$L/r.mjs\"` and `ln -s \"$PWD/$S\" \"$L/s.mjs\"`, ch... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n169: - [ ] T069 Move the arena marker comment beside the write (`ARB`). The three comment lines starting `// The runner refuses a live arm in a checkout without this file` sit above `const MARK = 'skilled-behaviour-arena';`. Move them, wording unchanged and indented two spaces, to sit directly above the `writeFileSync` call in `buildArena` that writes the marker under `join(dest, '.git', MARK)`. Leave one blank line between the `GIT_PREFIX` constant and the `MARK` constant. Change no code. Verify: `node --check $ARB` exits 0, `rg -c \"skilled-behaviour-arena\" $ARB` prints 1 and `rg -n -A1 \"its copied build trees\\.\" $ARB` prints the comment line and then the line holding `writeFileSync(join(dest, '.git', MARK)`.\n170: - [ ] T070 Read the Pi session header from the contained path (`$SR/pi.mjs`, `$SRT/pi.test.mjs`, `$SR/*.d.mts`, `$SR/*.d.mts.map`). `diskResult` opens the session file for its header with `headerOf(filePath)` before `guardSessionPath` checks that the path stays inside the store, so a session file that links out of the store is opened before the refusal. Test first: in `$SRT/pi.test.mjs`, add `test('a session file linked outside the store is refused without opening it', ...)` inside `withTempAsync('pi-outside', ...)`. Build `piSession({ root, projectDir })` after making `projectDir` as the other cases do. Write `join(root, 'outside-pi.jsonl')` with `writeJsonl`, holding the header `{ type: 'session', version: 3, id: SESSION, timestamp: '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z', cwd: projectDir }` and one user `message` row shaped like the fixture's `pi-u1` row. Replace `fixture.filePath` with a link to it, `rmSync(fixture.filePath)` and then `symlinkSync(outside, fixture.filePath)`. Start `trackFileOpens()`, call `readSession({ runtime: 'pi', sessionId: SESSION, ...fixture.options })` and call `tracker.restore()` in `finally`. Expect `ok` to be `false`, `code` to be `session_mismatch` and `tracker.opened` to hold neither `fixture.filePath` nor the outside path. Import `rmSync` and `symlinkSync` from `node:fs` and `trackFileOpens` and `writeJsonl` from `./helpers.mjs`. Today the code matches and the open check fails, because `headerOf` opens the link. Then in `diskResult`, right after `filePath` is built, call `resolveInside({ path: filePath, root })` and return its result when it is not `ok`. Read the header with `headerOf(contained.path)` and pass `contained.path` as `path` to `guardSessionPath`. Import `resolveInside` beside `guardSessionPath` from `./identity.mjs`. Add a comment saying the header is read from the path the containment check resolved, so a link out of the store is refused before any byte of it is read. Then run `SRD` and do not edit a declaration by hand. Verify: ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n171: - [ ] T071 Name the owner skill, not the raw row id, on a needs-a-mode head (`RD`, `GT`). A `needsMode` recommendation whose id has a generated command row, such as `command-create-agent`, renders `command-create-agent (needs a mode)`, because `commandLabel` returns `null` for it and the head falls back to the raw id. Test first: in `GT`, tighten the case `renders needs a mode even when the id has a command row` so it also expects the head to hold `use sk-doc (needs a mode)` and not to hold `command-`. Today it fails, because the head reads `use command-create-agent (needs a mode)`. Then in `renderAdvisorBrief`, change the `topLabel` fallback from `sanitizeSkillLabel(metadataSkillLabel(result) ?? top.skill)` to `sanitizeSkillLabel(metadataSkillLabel(result) ?? (top.needsMode === true ? commandRowFor(top.skill)?.ownerSkillId : undefined) ?? top.skill)`. Add a comment saying a recommendation that needs a mode is named by the skill that owns its row, because the row id is an internal key the reader cannot run. Verify: before the change `VX tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` reports 1 failed, after it 0 failed, `VX tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n172: - [ ] T072 Fail the holdout check on a corpus that marks no holdout row (`SLT`). Start only after T041 ends, because WG9 owns this file until then. In the case `publishes no holdout row`, add `expect(holdoutIds.size, 'the corpus marks no holdout row, so this check would pass without testing anything').toBeGreaterThan(0)` right after `holdoutIds` is built and before the key checks. Add a comment saying an empty set would make the check pass on a corpus that no longer marks its holdout rows. It passes today and fails the day the `split` field goes. Read only `id` and `split` from `CORPUS` and never print a holdout prompt. Verify: `VX tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed and `rg -c \"holdoutIds.size\" $SLT` prints 1.\n173: - [ ] T073 Tighten three README sentences the verifier found loose (`RA`). Start only after T055 ends, because WG10 owns this file until then. In the runner row, replace the sentence `A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics and each runtime's own prefixes plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no other credential in the operator's shell reaches it.` with `A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics, the locale and XDG prefixes, each runtime's own prefixes and the Skilled switch prefixes, plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no GitHub token or git credential helper from the operator's shell reaches it.` In the capture row, change `the command attached to each row` to `the command attached to each published row`. In the `scorer-eval-baseline.json` row, change ``the fixture hashes and `metrics` for the full corpus, holdout, ambiguity slice, gold-none false fire and intent buckets`` to ``the fixture hashes and `metrics` for the full corpus, holdout, ambiguity slice, gold-none false fire, intent buckets and unknown count``, and add `` `capturedAtSha`,`` after `` `capturedAt`,`` in the same row. Change nothing else in the file. Verify: `rg -c \"the locale and XDG prefixes\" $RA` prints 1, `rg -c \"no other credential\" $RA` prints nothing, `rg -c \"attached to each published row\" $RA` prints 1, `rg -c \"capturedAtSha\" $RA` prints at least 1, `rg -c \"intent buckets and unknown count\" $RA` prints 1 and ``rg -c '^\\| `[^`]+\\.(mjs|py|jsonl|json)` \\|' $RA`` prints 21.\n174: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n175: \n176: ---\n177: \n178: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n179: ## Phase 3: Verification\n180: \n181: - [ ] T056 [C] Rebuild and run every suite (`$SP/after.txt`). Run `npm run build` in `ADV`, then `VALL`, `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1 npx vitest run tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts tests/hooks/skill-advisor-cli-fallback-envelope.vitest.ts tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts` in `ADV`, `VB`, `DALL` and `VSR`, and write each command with its result lines into `after.txt`. Verify: the build exits 0, `VALL` reports 0 failed with more passed tests than `$SP/before.txt`, the marker run reports 0 failed, `VB` reports `4 passed (4)` and `DALL` and `VSR` report `fail 0`.\n182: - [ ] T057 [C] Hold every floor (`$SP/after.txt`). Run `FLOOR`, `EVAL`, `TC`, `SRD --check`, `DERIVE`, `RV`, `SRM`, `G2SUM` and `HOLD`, write each beside its before line and end the file with `RESULT: every floor holds` only when it does. Verify: no `FLOOR` value and no `EVAL` value in its first three places is lower than its before line, the fourth `EVAL` value is no higher than 5, `TC`, `RV` and `SRM` exit 0, `SRD --check` prints `current`, `DERIVE` prints `fresh`, `G2SUM` equals its before line and `HOLD` prints `\"rows\":0`.\n183: - [ ] T058 [C] Rerun the reproductions (`$SP/repro-after.txt`). Run the six probes in `PL` after the build and append their output. Verify: the file holds `main returned 3`, `restored line: true`, `after: CLAUDE.md`, `plan exit=2`, a `ci.yml` line with equal digests, `undefined` on the no-edit, `/deep:ai-council` and both file-name lines of `probe-attach.mts`, `'COMMAND_POLICIES' in module -> false`, `(needs a mode)` on the first `probe-render.mjs` line and `(parent-named only)` on its second, and `probe-postwrite-foreign.cjs` prints 15 `apply exit 0` lines.\n184: - [ ] T059 [C] Build one arena and read its marker (`$SP/arena-smoke.txt`). Run `node $ARB --source . --dest <a fresh folder under mktemp -d>/arena`, then `test -f <arena>/.git/skilled-behaviour-arena` and `node -e \"import('./$R').then((m) => console.log(JSON.stringify(m.liveArmRefusal(process.argv[1]))))\" <arena>`, write the outputs to `arena-smoke.txt` and remove the temporary folder. Start no runtime in it. Verify: the builder prints `\"ok\":true`, the `test -f` exits 0, the refusal prints `null` and `test -e <the temporary folder>` exits 1 at the end.\n185: - [ ] T060 [C] Check the boundaries. Verify: `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- AGENTS.md specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/spec.md $ADV/lib/scorer/text.ts` exits 0, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- .skilled | rg \"^\\+.*(//|#|\\*).*(specs/|REQ-0|ADR-0|T0[0-9]{2}|phase [0-9]|WG[0-9]|DR[0-9]-P|R[0-9]-P[0-9]|iter[0-9]-P|Q[0-9]-)\"` prints nothing, the same `rg` over `CAG`, `BG`, `SUBT` and `DSCT` prints nothing, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --stat HEAD -- '*package.json' '*package-lock.json'` prints nothing, `rg -n \"COMMAND_POLICIES\" .skilled --glob '!**/node_modules/**'` prints nothing and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --short -- .skilled` lists only files named in this phase's tasks.\n186: - [ ] T061 [C] Write `implementation-summary.md` and mark each row in `acceptance-criteria.md` with its evidence (`implementation-summary.md`, `acceptance-criteria.md`). Run `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/repair-derived.cjs --folder <this folder> --apply` first. Verify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <this folder> --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED`.\n187: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n188: \n189: ---\n190: \n191: <!-- ANCHOR:completion -->\n192: ## Completion Criteria\n193: \n194: - [ ] All tasks marked `[x]`\n195: - [ ] No `[B]` blocked tasks remaining\n196: - [ ] `$SP/after.txt` ends `RESULT: every floor holds` and `$SP/repro-after.txt` holds every fixed output T058 names\n197: <!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n198: \n199: ---\n200: \n201: <!-- ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n202: ## Cross-References\n203: \n204: - **Specification**: See `spec.md`\n205: - **Plan**: See `plan.md`\n206: - **Acceptance criteria**: See `acceptance-criteria.md`\n207: - **Review report**: `../review/review-report.md`\n208: <!-- /ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n209: \n210: ---\n211: \n212: ## Verification Checklist\n213: \n214: <!-- ANCHOR:protocol -->\n215: ## Verification Protocol\n216: \n217: | Priority | Handling | Completion Impact |\n218: |----------|----------|-------------------|\n219: | **[P0]** | HARD BLOCKER | Cannot claim done until complete |\n220: | **[P1]** | Required | Must complete OR get user approval |\n221: | **[P2]** | Optional | Can defer with documented reason |\n222: \n223: A blocked task gets `[B]` and one line naming what blocks it. A moved floor blocks the phase.\n224: <!-- /ANCHOR:protocol -->\n225: \n226: ---\n227: \n228: <!-- ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n229: ## Pre-Implementation\n230: \n231: - [ ] CHK-001 [P0] The before numbers and the reproductions are in `scratch/` (T001, T002), and `rg -c \"^order:\" scratch/before.txt` prints 1 (T003).\n232: - [ ] CHK-002 [P0] Each task line ends with a verify clause: `rg -c '^- \\[[ xB]\\] T[0-9].*Verify: ' tasks.md` and `rg -c '^- \\[[ xB]\\] T[0-9]' tasks.md` print the same number.\n233: <!-- /ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n234: \n235: ---\n236: \n237: <!-- ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n238: ## Code Quality\n239: \n240: - [ ] CHK-010 [P0] `TC` exits 0 and `node --check` passes on every changed `.mjs` and `.cjs` file.\n241: - [ ] CHK-011 [P0] No code comment names a spec path, a phase number, a task id, a finding id, an ADR or a REQ id (T060).\n242: - [ ] CHK-012 [P0] The generated block in `CA`, the baseline and the reader declarations are written by their generators only: `DERIVE` prints `fresh`, `SRD --check` prints `current` and `SLT` passes on the recaptured baseline.\n243: - [ ] CHK-013 [P1] `CA` still has no import: `rg -c \"^import\" $CA` prints nothing.\n244: <!-- /ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n245: \n246: ---\n247: \n248: <!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n249: ## Testing Checklist\n250: \n251: - [ ] CHK-020 [P0] Every floor holds (T057): `scratch/after.txt` ends `RESULT: every floor holds`.\n252: - [ ] CHK-021 [P0] Each fix's verify recorded its test failing before the change and passing after, except the cases the spec names as passing today.\n253: - [ ] CHK-022 [P0] The two former P1 reproductions print the fixed output (T058).\n254: - [ ] CHK-023 [P0] The renderer tests pass under `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1` (T056).\n255: - [ ] CHK-024 [P1] One arena built by the builder carries the marker and passes `liveArmRefusal` (T059).\n256: <!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n257: \n258: ---\n259: \n260: <!-- ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n261: ## FIX ADDENDUM: FIX COMPLETENESS\n262: \n263: - [ ] CHK-FIX-001 [P0] Each finding has a class: embed write fault and restore `algorithmic`, linked router or mirror `class-of-bug` (the root-document rule extended to every written path), foreign failure `instance-only`, the attach guards `cross-consumer` (attach path, scorer, capture), `COMMAND_POLICIES` `dead generated surface`, renderer heads `instance-only`, renderer tests `test-isolation`, env allowlist and arena marker `class-of-bug`, g2 labels `duplicated source of truth`, the check, workflow and README findings `matrix/evidence` or doc drift, the readers `instance-only` except the containment path `class-of-bug` across four readers.\n264: - [ ] CHK-FIX-002 [P0] Same-class producers: `rg -n \"NO_EDIT_REQUEST\" $ADV/lib` lists `CA` and `FU` only, with `scorer/text.ts` holding the recorded third copy, and `rg -n \"isSymlink\\(\" $EMB` lists the root, router, mirror and `CLAUDE.md` checks.\n265: - [ ] CHK-FIX-003 [P0] Consumers of every changed symbol are listed in `plan.md` and the consumer `rg` in its required inventories prints no unlisted file (T060).\n266: - [ ] CHK-FIX-004 [P0] The path and parser fixes carry adversarial cases: a link to an in-root file, a partial and a complete linked router, a file name with `.` and with `/`, a sentence-ending period and a comma, a typo `/deep:` command, a router row with a literal pipe and a marker named only in a description.\n267: - [ ] CHK-FIX-005 [P1] The matrix axes in `plan.md` each have their rows in the tests T004 to T011, T021, T022, T027 and T031 add.\n268: - [ ] CHK-FIX-006 [P1] The hostile env variant ran: the renderer tests under `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1` and the env allowlist test with secrets stubbed.\n269: - [ ] CHK-FIX-007 [P1] Evidence names the commit it ran on: `scratch/after.txt` and `implementation-summary.md` name the base commit with the changes in the working tree.\n270: <!-- /ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 271]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/014-review-remediation/tasks.md\",\"offset\":151,\"limit\":120}"},"time":{"start":1794561533789,"end":1794561534820}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794561534832,"sessionID":"ses_e2bccaf18ffeU7jQUyMBm6GhmF","part":{"partID":"prt_1d433e9e2001XwliVYf9Le4ADI","sessionID":"ses_e2bccaf18ffeU7jQUyMBm6GhmF","messageID":"msg_1d433c91400174Y41pz9XXKVRo","type":"tool","id":"call_71fde7b2a56a48478120b566","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md, lines 1-195\n1: ---\n2: title: \"system skill advisor runtime scripts routing accuracy: Code README\"\n3: description: \"Code-facing README for .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"system-skill-advisor runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy\"\n6:   - \"code README\"\n7: ---\n8: \n9: # system skill advisor runtime scripts routing accuracy\n10: \n11: Operator and maintenance scripts for this skill.\n12: \n13: ---\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: ### Purpose\n18: \n19: This README documents the code-bearing folder `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy` so operators can understand its role without opening every source file first. It follows the sk-doc skill README structure while staying focused on code navigation.\n20: \n21: ### Usage\n22: \n23: Use this file to identify the folder boundary, the likely verification path and the local source files that need sk-code conventions. Keep behavior details in source comments and higher-level workflow details in the owning `SKILL.md`.\n24: \n25: ### Key Statistics\n26: \n27: | Metric | Value |\n28: |---|---:|\n29: | Code files | 14 |\n30: | README scope | Direct files in this folder |\n31: | Audit context | Internal validation notes |\n32: \n33: ---\n34: \n35: ## 2. QUICK START\n36: \n37: **Step 1: Confirm the owner.**\n38: \n39: Start with `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md` for runtime routing and workflow boundaries.\n40: \n41: **Step 2: Inspect the local code.**\n42: \n43: ```bash\n44: rg --files .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy\n45: ```\n46: \n47: Expected result: the command lists the source files summarized below.\n48: \n49: **Step 3: Verify changes.**\n50: \n51: Run individual scripts from the repository root with the documented arguments.\n52: \n53: ---\n54: \n55: ## 3. FEATURES\n56: \n57: | Feature | What It Does |\n58: |---|---|\n59: | Folder boundary | Documents direct code files under `runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy`. |\n60: | sk-code alignment | Points reviewers at OpenCode naming, header, error-handling and type-discipline checks. |\n61: | Verification handoff | Records the expected owner and audit packet for follow-up work. |\n62: \n63: ### Attribution on every run line\n64: \n65: Each line a behaviour run appends carries three fields beside the locator, exit and changed paths:\n66: \n67: - `offeredCommand` is the slash command the advisor head named, or `null` when the head named none.\n68: - `offeredPolicy` is the policy that directly follows that command, one of `run`, `start` or `ask`, or `null` when no policy follows it.\n69: - `briefDelivered` says whether the advisor brief reached the session. It is `true` or `false` where the runtime keeps a readable record, and `unknown` where none exists or cannot be read, so a gap in the record never reads as a miss.\n70: \n71: The offer is a replay of the Claude prompt hook after the turn instead of a copy of the live session head. The head is the only text that names both the command and the policy, and not every runtime keeps it, so `replayOffer` runs the checkout's own hook and records what that checkout would have offered at the time of the run. A replay that finds no hook, a failing hook or a head without an offer writes null fields plus an `offerError` of `hook_missing`, `hook_failed` or `no_head`, so one bad line never aborts a run.\n72: \n73: ### Backfill a finished log\n74: \n75: `run-command-behaviour.mjs --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log>` adds those three fields to a log written before they existed, and a `--backfill` run without `--out` is a usage error that exits 2. The mode refuses with exit 2 and a JSON body holding its `code`:\n76: \n77: - `same_path` when `--out` resolves to the input log, because the backfill writes a new file.\n78: - `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when the corpus refuses a row id, because every prompt is resolved before the first line is written and a refused row leaves no output file behind.\n79: \n80: A line whose locator names no checkout keeps null offer fields and `offerError` `no_checkout`, and the rest of the log is still written with `offerBackfilled: true`.\n81: \n82: `--brief-only` keeps every offer field exactly as the input line holds it, present or absent, and recomputes only `briefDelivered`, because a recorded offer belongs to the head the turn saw and replaying it would describe a different head. Brief delivery now comes from the session records of Pi, Devin, DSH and Codex, while OpenCode stays `unknown` because its plugin adds the brief in memory and the store keeps no copy.\n83: \n84: ### The typed attachment block\n85: \n86: `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` reports typed reach twice under `attachedMetrics`:\n87: \n88: - `attachedMetrics.typed` counts `own`, `attached`, `combined` and `total` across the typed rows. `own` is a row the ranked bridge already landed, `attached` is a row the typed command itself delivered, and `combined` is their sum.\n89: - `attachedMetrics.typedRows` keys one record per typed row id in sorted order with `expected_command`, `top`, `carried`, `command` and `policy`. `carried` is `own`, `attached` or null, so a missed row names the command it did carry instead of only lowering a count.\n90: \n91: A typed command travels on its owner. It is delivered only when the leading recommendation is that command's owner skill or carries the same command row, so an unrelated leading skill never presents it. A typed command with no scorer row of its own has no other path to the reader, so its owner is ranked first.\n92: \n93: ### Measure the brief cost\n94: \n95: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/measure-brief-cost.mjs` reports what the Claude prompt hook actually sends the model for a typed row.\n96: \n97: - Flags: `node measure-brief-cost.mjs [--rows <id,id>] [--root <dir>]` keeps every typed row on the `none` split, `--rows` narrows the run to named corpus ids and `--root` names the checkout the corpus and hook are read from, defaulting to the current directory. A holdout or unknown id exits 2 before anything is spawned, and no prompt is ever printed.\n98: - Output keys: one JSON object holds `rows`, then `totalBytes`, `medianBytes` and `maxBytes`. Each entry of `rows` carries `id`, `bytes` and `estimatedTokens`, where bytes are the UTF-8 length of `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` and tokens divide that by four, the renderer's own characters per token.\n99: - Sandbox: the run makes one `ssa-cost-` directory under the OS temp dir and sets `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` to it for every hook process, so the operator's warm daemon is never reached. At the end it stops only a process whose command line names that directory and removes it.\n100: \n101: ---\n102: \n103: ## 4. STRUCTURE\n104: \n105: | Path | Purpose |\n106: |---|---|\n107: | `gate3-corpus-runner.mjs` | MJS source file in this folder. |\n108: | `score-routing-corpus.py` | PY source file in this folder. |\n109: | `command-prompts.jsonl` | Prompt corpus with a gold slash command per row, covering every tracked command. |\n110: | `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Scores `command-prompts.jsonl` under the pinned regime and prints or writes the baseline. Its `attachedMetrics` key holds own-row, attached and combined plain reach per split and per command family, attached false offers, the typed rows where a command and its owner skill read as ambiguous, and the command attached to each row. An attached hit counts only on a row the ranked bridge did not already land. Its `rows`, `attachedRows` and `typedRows` maps leave out every holdout row, so only holdout counts are published. The optional `--real-embedding` flag scores the same corpus against this machine's own skill graph database and live embedder in place of the fixture vectors and word overlap the default run substitutes, so its numbers describe an environment rather than a reproducible projection. It refuses `--write` because the committed baseline comes only from the fixture regime. Before scoring it proves two things, that a live embedder answers and serves the `nomic-embed-text` model family the stored vectors were built with, and that the skill graph database holds a populated `vec_cmd_*` command vector table. A missing piece exits 2 with a message that names it. Each row in its `rows` output also carries `offered`, which is true when any of the top three recommendations maps to a command bridge, so it measures reach rather than whether a brief head offered the command. |\n111: | `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. `--row-id <id>` narrows either mode to one row and `--json` prints machine-readable output. Each per-arm block reports `startRate`, `cappedStartRows`, `offered`, `cappedOfferRows`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerReached`, `deliveryUnknown`, `offerSeen`, `offerDeclined`, `offerFieldAbsent`, `unsafe`, `armIncomplete` and `byPolicy`, where `byPolicy` holds a `run`, `start` and `ask` tally of rows, capped rows, started rows and rows that wrote. `cappedOfferRows` counts offers whose turn hit its cap, `offerReached` counts offers whose brief was not shown missing, so a row whose brief state is unknown counts, `deliveryUnknown` counts rows whose brief state is `unknown`, `offerSeen` and `offerDeclined` read the session's `COMMAND:` lines, and `offerFieldAbsent` counts rows that carry no offer field. `unsafe` also counts a workflow loop that started without its init, and a Claude turn that keeps only the `<command-name>` tag of a typed command counts as a start because Claude Code expands that command before the model sees it. The text output prints one `policy` line per policy under each arm. Exit 2 means nothing was scored: bad flags, a missing or malformed run log (`run_log_missing`, `run_log_malformed`), or a reader refusal printed with its own code such as `capability_gated` or `locator_unavailable`. |\n112: | `command-offer.mjs` | Recovers the attribution fields for a run line. `parseOffer` reads the command and policy out of one head, `replayOffer` replays a checkout's Claude prompt hook after the turn, `briefDelivered` reads the session record, and `backfillRunLog` writes the fields onto a finished log with a refusal code for a holdout, unknown or same-path input. |\n113: | `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`, `--out <run-log>`, optional `--arm`, `--fixture` and `--dry-run`. `--backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]` rewrites a finished log with `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy` and `briefDelivered` instead of running a turn, and prints a JSON refusal with `same_path`, `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when it cannot. After each turn it restores the checkout to HEAD and reports any `leftoverPaths` whose status still differs from the snapshot taken before the turn. Exit 3 (`head_moved`) ends the arm without that restore, because a commit or a stash made during the turn is not the runner's to discard, so the line records `restored: false` and the changed paths stay listed. A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics and each runtime's own prefixes plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no other credential in the operator's shell reaches it. Exit 2 refuses instead of running: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout or unknown row, a set with fewer rows than it needs (`set_short`), a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder (`worktree_dirty`), a checkout with a git remote (`remote_present`), a checkout without the arena builder's marker (`arena_unprepared`), or a Codex arm whose project hook is unapproved (`codex-hooks-unapproved`). |\n114: | `command-routing-baseline.json` | Captured command routing numbers that `tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` holds the scorer to. |\n115: | `build-holdout.mjs` | Builds the frozen holdout from three separately authored fixtures that are disjoint from the labeled corpus, copying each row's own label and keeping its provenance in `source_type` and `origin_fixture`. Family-normalized dedup keeps the first occurrence and drops any prompt that collides with the training corpus. `--write` rewrites `holdout-prompts.jsonl`. |\n116: | `capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs` | Recomputes the strict Python/native top-1 divergence set the ratchet replays. An existing approval is kept only while its prompt hash and both current tops still match, so the output is a capture of live scoring rather than a hand-edited fixture. Scores both scorers against a skill graph indexed fresh into a temporary directory with the clock pinned to the capture day, and `--write` records the result. |\n117: | `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs` | Captures scorer accuracy across the full corpus, the independent holdout, the frozen ambiguity slice and the named intent buckets under the reproducible filesystem projection, then `--write` records `scorer-eval-baseline.json`. Fixture hashes pin the baseline to the exact corpora it was captured against, so editing a fixture forces a conscious re-capture. `--misses` prints one failing prompt per line. |\n118: | `derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs` | Derives the frozen ambiguity slice as the corpus rows whose top-2 fused-score margin is at or below `tau`, keeping each row's existing gold label, so a regression that only hurts contested prompts still fails a gate. `--write` rewrites `ambiguity-prompts.jsonl`, and the frozen `margin_at_capture` documents why a row is in the slice without being trusted by the gate. |\n119: | `prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` | Builds the arena a live arm runs in: a clone of the branch tip with no remote and no outer repository, plus the ignored build trees copied in and stamped at copy time so the freshness guard reads them as fresh. It refuses a destination inside a repository or one that already exists, and skips every file whose name begins with `.env` so no secret follows the build output in. |\n120: | `reject-unknown-options.mjs` | Shared argument guard that exits 2 on the first token outside a script's declared option list, because silently ignoring a misspelled flag would run the default behavior and make the no-op look successful. |\n121: | `score-outcome-rerank.mjs` | Measures whether the outcome-weighted rerank improves routing over the similarity-only ordering, reported as MRR and right-skill@3 rather than @1 alone. The reliability fold is built on a train split of the labeled corpus and accuracy is measured only on a disjoint held-out split, so memorized labels do not transfer. Read-only against the skill graph. |\n122: | `worktree-restore.mjs` | Restores one measured turn's checkout to the snapshot taken before it, leaving a path that was already dirty alone and dropping a staged path HEAD does not know. A second snapshot after the restore reports what still differs, so the caller fails the run instead of claiming a clean worktree. |\n123: | `ambiguity-prompts.jsonl` | Frozen ambiguity slice written by `derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs` and read by `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs`. Each row carries `id`, `prompt`, `skill_top_1`, `bucket`, `source_type`, `tau`, `margin_at_capture` and `captured_at`. |\n124: | `doctor-prompts.jsonl` | Doctor routing slice read by `tests/doctor-routing-slice.vitest.ts`. Each row carries `id`, `prompt`, `expected_top`, `expected_second` and `wave`, where a wave scores only after every doctor bridge its rows expect is active, so a half-shipped bridge cannot fail the gate. |\n125: | `holdout-prompts.jsonl` | Frozen holdout written by `build-holdout.mjs` from three separately authored fixtures disjoint from the labeled corpus, and read by `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs` as its unseen split. |\n126: | `labeled-prompts.jsonl` | Labeled training corpus with `id`, `prompt`, `skill_top_1`, `skill_correct`, `bucket`, `source_type`, `notes`, `gate3_triggers` and `gate3_reason_category`. Scored by `score-routing-corpus.py`, `score-outcome-rerank.mjs` and `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs`, and read by the divergence ledger, the ambiguity deriver, the holdout builder and the gate 3 corpus runner. |\n127: | `scorer-eval-baseline.json` | Scorer eval baseline written by `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs`: `schemaVersion`, `capturedAt`, `env`, the fixture hashes and `metrics` for the full corpus, holdout, ambiguity slice, gold-none false fire and intent buckets. The ratchet gate re-scores live under the same environment and holds every metric to it. |\n128: \n129: ---\n130: \n131: ## 5. CONFIGURATION\n132: \n133: | Setting | Default | Purpose |\n134: |---|---|---|\n135: | sk-code surface | OPENCODE | Applies OpenCode TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Shell and config conventions. |\n136: | README scope | Direct folder | This file documents this folder, not sibling folders. |\n137: \n138: ---\n139: \n140: ## 6. USAGE EXAMPLES\n141: \n142: **Audit this folder**\n143: \n144: ```text\n145: User request: Check .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy for sk-code and README coverage.\n146: Skill routing: sk-code plus sk-doc.\n147: Expected output: Findings recorded in the 026 audit report.\n148: ```\n149: \n150: **Score captured runs**\n151: \n152: ```bash\n153: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs \\\n154:   --summary scratch/runs-pre.jsonl scratch/runs-post.jsonl --json\n155: ```\n156: \n157: Exit 2 means no score was produced. A missing or malformed log prints `run_log_missing` or `run_log_malformed`, and a session the reader cannot open prints its own code, such as `capability_gated`.\n158: \n159: **Inspect and run one behaviour turn**\n160: \n161: ```bash\n162: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv claude\n163: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs \\\n164:   --runtime claude --set chat --out scratch/runs-pre.jsonl --dry-run\n165: ```\n166: \n167: Exit 2 means the run was refused before or during its first prompt: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout row, a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder, or a checkout that still has a git remote.\n168: \n169: **Backfill a finished log**\n170: \n171: ```bash\n172: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs \\\n173:   --backfill scratch/runs-pre.jsonl --out scratch/runs-pre-attributed.jsonl\n174: ```\n175: \n176: The output log holds the same lines plus `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offerError`, `briefDelivered` and `offerBackfilled`. Exit 2 means the output was refused, and the printed JSON names why: `same_path`, `holdout_row`, `unknown_row` or a missing `--out`.\n177: \n178: ---\n179: \n180: ## 7. TROUBLESHOOTING\n181: \n182: | What You See | Cause | Fix |\n183: |---|---|---|\n184: | README appears stale | Source files changed after this audit | Refresh the structure table and rerun the 026 audit check. |\n185: | Verification command is unclear | Folder is a helper boundary | Use the nearest package or skill-level verification command. |\n186: \n187: ---\n188: \n189: ## 8. RELATED DOCUMENTS\n190: \n191: | Document | Purpose |\n192: |---|---|\n193: | [`system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md`](../../../SKILL.md) | Runtime instructions for the owning skill. |\n194: | [`sk-code/SKILL.md`](../../../../sk-code/SKILL.md) | OpenCode coding standards and verification routing. |\n195: | [`sk-doc skill-readme-template.md`](../../../../sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-readme-template.md) | README structure used for this code README. |","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md, lines 1-195\n1: ---\n2: title: \"system skill advisor runtime scripts routing accuracy: Code README\"\n3: description: \"Code-facing README for .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"system-skill-advisor runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy\"\n6:   - \"code README\"\n7: ---\n8: \n9: # system skill advisor runtime scripts routing accuracy\n10: \n11: Operator and maintenance scripts for this skill.\n12: \n13: ---\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: ### Purpose\n18: \n19: This README documents the code-bearing folder `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy` so operators can understand its role without opening every source file first. It follows the sk-doc skill README structure while staying focused on code navigation.\n20: \n21: ### Usage\n22: \n23: Use this file to identify the folder boundary, the likely verification path and the local source files that need sk-code conventions. Keep behavior details in source comments and higher-level workflow details in the owning `SKILL.md`.\n24: \n25: ### Key Statistics\n26: \n27: | Metric | Value |\n28: |---|---:|\n29: | Code files | 14 |\n30: | README scope | Direct files in this folder |\n31: | Audit context | Internal validation notes |\n32: \n33: ---\n34: \n35: ## 2. QUICK START\n36: \n37: **Step 1: Confirm the owner.**\n38: \n39: Start with `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md` for runtime routing and workflow boundaries.\n40: \n41: **Step 2: Inspect the local code.**\n42: \n43: ```bash\n44: rg --files .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy\n45: ```\n46: \n47: Expected result: the command lists the source files summarized below.\n48: \n49: **Step 3: Verify changes.**\n50: \n51: Run individual scripts from the repository root with the documented arguments.\n52: \n53: ---\n54: \n55: ## 3. FEATURES\n56: \n57: | Feature | What It Does |\n58: |---|---|\n59: | Folder boundary | Documents direct code files under `runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy`. |\n60: | sk-code alignment | Points reviewers at OpenCode naming, header, error-handling and type-discipline checks. |\n61: | Verification handoff | Records the expected owner and audit packet for follow-up work. |\n62: \n63: ### Attribution on every run line\n64: \n65: Each line a behaviour run appends carries three fields beside the locator, exit and changed paths:\n66: \n67: - `offeredCommand` is the slash command the advisor head named, or `null` when the head named none.\n68: - `offeredPolicy` is the policy that directly follows that command, one of `run`, `start` or `ask`, or `null` when no policy follows it.\n69: - `briefDelivered` says whether the advisor brief reached the session. It is `true` or `false` where the runtime keeps a readable record, and `unknown` where none exists or cannot be read, so a gap in the record never reads as a miss.\n70: \n71: The offer is a replay of the Claude prompt hook after the turn instead of a copy of the live session head. The head is the only text that names both the command and the policy, and not every runtime keeps it, so `replayOffer` runs the checkout's own hook and records what that checkout would have offered at the time of the run. A replay that finds no hook, a failing hook or a head without an offer writes null fields plus an `offerError` of `hook_missing`, `hook_failed` or `no_head`, so one bad line never aborts a run.\n72: \n73: ### Backfill a finished log\n74: \n75: `run-command-behaviour.mjs --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log>` adds those three fields to a log written before they existed, and a `--backfill` run without `--out` is a usage error that exits 2. The mode refuses with exit 2 and a JSON body holding its `code`:\n76: \n77: - `same_path` when `--out` resolves to the input log, because the backfill writes a new file.\n78: - `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when the corpus refuses a row id, because every prompt is resolved before the first line is written and a refused row leaves no output file behind.\n79: \n80: A line whose locator names no checkout keeps null offer fields and `offerError` `no_checkout`, and the rest of the log is still written with `offerBackfilled: true`.\n81: \n82: `--brief-only` keeps every offer field exactly as the input line holds it, present or absent, and recomputes only `briefDelivered`, because a recorded offer belongs to the head the turn saw and replaying it would describe a different head. Brief delivery now comes from the session records of Pi, Devin, DSH and Codex, while OpenCode stays `unknown` because its plugin adds the brief in memory and the store keeps no copy.\n83: \n84: ### The typed attachment block\n85: \n86: `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` reports typed reach twice under `attachedMetrics`:\n87: \n88: - `attachedMetrics.typed` counts `own`, `attached`, `combined` and `total` across the typed rows. `own` is a row the ranked bridge already landed, `attached` is a row the typed command itself delivered, and `combined` is their sum.\n89: - `attachedMetrics.typedRows` keys one record per typed row id in sorted order with `expected_command`, `top`, `carried`, `command` and `policy`. `carried` is `own`, `attached` or null, so a missed row names the command it did carry instead of only lowering a count.\n90: \n91: A typed command travels on its owner. It is delivered only when the leading recommendation is that command's owner skill or carries the same command row, so an unrelated leading skill never presents it. A typed command with no scorer row of its own has no other path to the reader, so its owner is ranked first.\n92: \n93: ### Measure the brief cost\n94: \n95: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/measure-brief-cost.mjs` reports what the Claude prompt hook actually sends the model for a typed row.\n96: \n97: - Flags: `node measure-brief-cost.mjs [--rows <id,id>] [--root <dir>]` keeps every typed row on the `none` split, `--rows` narrows the run to named corpus ids and `--root` names the checkout the corpus and hook are read from, defaulting to the current directory. A holdout or unknown id exits 2 before anything is spawned, and no prompt is ever printed.\n98: - Output keys: one JSON object holds `rows`, then `totalBytes`, `medianBytes` and `maxBytes`. Each entry of `rows` carries `id`, `bytes` and `estimatedTokens`, where bytes are the UTF-8 length of `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` and tokens divide that by four, the renderer's own characters per token.\n99: - Sandbox: the run makes one `ssa-cost-` directory under the OS temp dir and sets `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` to it for every hook process, so the operator's warm daemon is never reached. At the end it stops only a process whose command line names that directory and removes it.\n100: \n101: ---\n102: \n103: ## 4. STRUCTURE\n104: \n105: | Path | Purpose |\n106: |---|---|\n107: | `gate3-corpus-runner.mjs` | MJS source file in this folder. |\n108: | `score-routing-corpus.py` | PY source file in this folder. |\n109: | `command-prompts.jsonl` | Prompt corpus with a gold slash command per row, covering every tracked command. |\n110: | `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Scores `command-prompts.jsonl` under the pinned regime and prints or writes the baseline. Its `attachedMetrics` key holds own-row, attached and combined plain reach per split and per command family, attached false offers, the typed rows where a command and its owner skill read as ambiguous, and the command attached to each row. An attached hit counts only on a row the ranked bridge did not already land. Its `rows`, `attachedRows` and `typedRows` maps leave out every holdout row, so only holdout counts are published. The optional `--real-embedding` flag scores the same corpus against this machine's own skill graph database and live embedder in place of the fixture vectors and word overlap the default run substitutes, so its numbers describe an environment rather than a reproducible projection. It refuses `--write` because the committed baseline comes only from the fixture regime. Before scoring it proves two things, that a live embedder answers and serves the `nomic-embed-text` model family the stored vectors were built with, and that the skill graph database holds a populated `vec_cmd_*` command vector table. A missing piece exits 2 with a message that names it. Each row in its `rows` output also carries `offered`, which is true when any of the top three recommendations maps to a command bridge, so it measures reach rather than whether a brief head offered the command. |\n111: | `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. `--row-id <id>` narrows either mode to one row and `--json` prints machine-readable output. Each per-arm block reports `startRate`, `cappedStartRows`, `offered`, `cappedOfferRows`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerReached`, `deliveryUnknown`, `offerSeen`, `offerDeclined`, `offerFieldAbsent`, `unsafe`, `armIncomplete` and `byPolicy`, where `byPolicy` holds a `run`, `start` and `ask` tally of rows, capped rows, started rows and rows that wrote. `cappedOfferRows` counts offers whose turn hit its cap, `offerReached` counts offers whose brief was not shown missing, so a row whose brief state is unknown counts, `deliveryUnknown` counts rows whose brief state is `unknown`, `offerSeen` and `offerDeclined` read the session's `COMMAND:` lines, and `offerFieldAbsent` counts rows that carry no offer field. `unsafe` also counts a workflow loop that started without its init, and a Claude turn that keeps only the `<command-name>` tag of a typed command counts as a start because Claude Code expands that command before the model sees it. The text output prints one `policy` line per policy under each arm. Exit 2 means nothing was scored: bad flags, a missing or malformed run log (`run_log_missing`, `run_log_malformed`), or a reader refusal printed with its own code such as `capability_gated` or `locator_unavailable`. |\n112: | `command-offer.mjs` | Recovers the attribution fields for a run line. `parseOffer` reads the command and policy out of one head, `replayOffer` replays a checkout's Claude prompt hook after the turn, `briefDelivered` reads the session record, and `backfillRunLog` writes the fields onto a finished log with a refusal code for a holdout, unknown or same-path input. |\n113: | `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`, `--out <run-log>`, optional `--arm`, `--fixture` and `--dry-run`. `--backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]` rewrites a finished log with `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy` and `briefDelivered` instead of running a turn, and prints a JSON refusal with `same_path`, `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when it cannot. After each turn it restores the checkout to HEAD and reports any `leftoverPaths` whose status still differs from the snapshot taken before the turn. Exit 3 (`head_moved`) ends the arm without that restore, because a commit or a stash made during the turn is not the runner's to discard, so the line records `restored: false` and the changed paths stay listed. A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics and each runtime's own prefixes plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no other credential in the operator's shell reaches it. Exit 2 refuses instead of running: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout or unknown row, a set with fewer rows than it needs (`set_short`), a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder (`worktree_dirty`), a checkout with a git remote (`remote_present`), a checkout without the arena builder's marker (`arena_unprepared`), or a Codex arm whose project hook is unapproved (`codex-hooks-unapproved`). |\n114: | `command-routing-baseline.json` | Captured command routing numbers that `tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` holds the scorer to. |\n115: | `build-holdout.mjs` | Builds the frozen holdout from three separately authored fixtures that are disjoint from the labeled corpus, copying each row's own label and keeping its provenance in `source_type` and `origin_fixture`. Family-normalized dedup keeps the first occurrence and drops any prompt that collides with the training corpus. `--write` rewrites `holdout-prompts.jsonl`. |\n116: | `capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs` | Recomputes the strict Python/native top-1 divergence set the ratchet replays. An existing approval is kept only while its prompt hash and both current tops still match, so the output is a capture of live scoring rather than a hand-edited fixture. Scores both scorers against a skill graph indexed fresh into a temporary directory with the clock pinned to the capture day, and `--write` records the result. |\n117: | `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs` | Captures scorer accuracy across the full corpus, the independent holdout, the frozen ambiguity slice and the named intent buckets under the reproducible filesystem projection, then `--write` records `scorer-eval-baseline.json`. Fixture hashes pin the baseline to the exact corpora it was captured against, so editing a fixture forces a conscious re-capture. `--misses` prints one failing prompt per line. |\n118: | `derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs` | Derives the frozen ambiguity slice as the corpus rows whose top-2 fused-score margin is at or below `tau`, keeping each row's existing gold label, so a regression that only hurts contested prompts still fails a gate. `--write` rewrites `ambiguity-prompts.jsonl`, and the frozen `margin_at_capture` documents why a row is in the slice without being trusted by the gate. |\n119: | `prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` | Builds the arena a live arm runs in: a clone of the branch tip with no remote and no outer repository, plus the ignored build trees copied in and stamped at copy time so the freshness guard reads them as fresh. It refuses a destination inside a repository or one that already exists, and skips every file whose name begins with `.env` so no secret follows the build output in. |\n120: | `reject-unknown-options.mjs` | Shared argument guard that exits 2 on the first token outside a script's declared option list, because silently ignoring a misspelled flag would run the default behavior and make the no-op look successful. |\n121: | `score-outcome-rerank.mjs` | Measures whether the outcome-weighted rerank improves routing over the similarity-only ordering, reported as MRR and right-skill@3 rather than @1 alone. The reliability fold is built on a train split of the labeled corpus and accuracy is measured only on a disjoint held-out split, so memorized labels do not transfer. Read-only against the skill graph. |\n122: | `worktree-restore.mjs` | Restores one measured turn's checkout to the snapshot taken before it, leaving a path that was already dirty alone and dropping a staged path HEAD does not know. A second snapshot after the restore reports what still differs, so the caller fails the run instead of claiming a clean worktree. |\n123: | `ambiguity-prompts.jsonl` | Frozen ambiguity slice written by `derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs` and read by `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs`. Each row carries `id`, `prompt`, `skill_top_1`, `bucket`, `source_type`, `tau`, `margin_at_capture` and `captured_at`. |\n124: | `doctor-prompts.jsonl` | Doctor routing slice read by `tests/doctor-routing-slice.vitest.ts`. Each row carries `id`, `prompt`, `expected_top`, `expected_second` and `wave`, where a wave scores only after every doctor bridge its rows expect is active, so a half-shipped bridge cannot fail the gate. |\n125: | `holdout-prompts.jsonl` | Frozen holdout written by `build-holdout.mjs` from three separately authored fixtures disjoint from the labeled corpus, and read by `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs` as its unseen split. |\n126: | `labeled-prompts.jsonl` | Labeled training corpus with `id`, `prompt`, `skill_top_1`, `skill_correct`, `bucket`, `source_type`, `notes`, `gate3_triggers` and `gate3_reason_category`. Scored by `score-routing-corpus.py`, `score-outcome-rerank.mjs` and `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs`, and read by the divergence ledger, the ambiguity deriver, the holdout builder and the gate 3 corpus runner. |\n127: | `scorer-eval-baseline.json` | Scorer eval baseline written by `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs`: `schemaVersion`, `capturedAt`, `env`, the fixture hashes and `metrics` for the full corpus, holdout, ambiguity slice, gold-none false fire and intent buckets. The ratchet gate re-scores live under the same environment and holds every metric to it. |\n128: \n129: ---\n130: \n131: ## 5. CONFIGURATION\n132: \n133: | Setting | Default | Purpose |\n134: |---|---|---|\n135: | sk-code surface | OPENCODE | Applies OpenCode TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Shell and config conventions. |\n136: | README scope | Direct folder | This file documents this folder, not sibling folders. |\n137: \n138: ---\n139: \n140: ## 6. USAGE EXAMPLES\n141: \n142: **Audit this folder**\n143: \n144: ```text\n145: User request: Check .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy for sk-code and README coverage.\n146: Skill routing: sk-code plus sk-doc.\n147: Expected output: Findings recorded in the 026 audit report.\n148: ```\n149: \n150: **Score captured runs**\n151: \n152: ```bash\n153: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs \\\n154:   --summary scratch/runs-pre.jsonl scratch/runs-post.jsonl --json\n155: ```\n156: \n157: Exit 2 means no score was produced. A missing or malformed log prints `run_log_missing` or `run_log_malformed`, and a session the reader cannot open prints its own code, such as `capability_gated`.\n158: \n159: **Inspect and run one behaviour turn**\n160: \n161: ```bash\n162: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv claude\n163: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs \\\n164:   --runtime claude --set chat --out scratch/runs-pre.jsonl --dry-run\n165: ```\n166: \n167: Exit 2 means the run was refused before or during its first prompt: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout row, a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder, or a checkout that still has a git remote.\n168: \n169: **Backfill a finished log**\n170: \n171: ```bash\n172: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs \\\n173:   --backfill scratch/runs-pre.jsonl --out scratch/runs-pre-attributed.jsonl\n174: ```\n175: \n176: The output log holds the same lines plus `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offerError`, `briefDelivered` and `offerBackfilled`. Exit 2 means the output was refused, and the printed JSON names why: `same_path`, `holdout_row`, `unknown_row` or a missing `--out`.\n177: \n178: ---\n179: \n180: ## 7. TROUBLESHOOTING\n181: \n182: | What You See | Cause | Fix |\n183: |---|---|---|\n184: | README appears stale | Source files changed after this audit | Refresh the structure table and rerun the 026 audit check. |\n185: | Verification command is unclear | Folder is a helper boundary | Use the nearest package or skill-level verification command. |\n186: \n187: ---\n188: \n189: ## 8. RELATED DOCUMENTS\n190: \n191: | Document | Purpose |\n192: |---|---|\n193: | [`system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md`](../../../SKILL.md) | Runtime instructions for the owning skill. |\n194: | [`sk-code/SKILL.md`](../../../../sk-code/SKILL.md) | OpenCode coding standards and verification routing. |\n195: | [`sk-doc skill-readme-template.md`](../../../../sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-readme-template.md) | README structure used for this code README. |"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794561534434,"end":1794561534832}}}}
